{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790404274,"nonce":"PXtFEloi5FnvO4EPVm/eIR+F21I=","title":"A function whose first-call compile fails aborts the process","message":"A stub standing in for a lazily compiled function calls `osr::lazy_compile`, which aborts (\"a function compiled on first call could not be compiled\") when the compile returns no entry: the stub has nothing else it can call, since no native entry runs a function in the interpreter. So any first-call compile failure reached from compiled code ends the process, while the same failure at a promotion keeps the function at its current tier (beadie drops the job and re-proposes later). The failures that reach it: a codegen error in the body, and a JIT module whose reserved address range is full (`jit_memory`, a 2 GiB range per module since the fix for c5ddf1f7ee2d6149eb09eae2cbb69a74ba6d35d8d3263e3be69a4519fe4d6a4b). Calls from the interpreter already fall back (`try_lazy_compile` returns null and the interpreter runs the body).\n\nFix direction: a native entry per call shape that marshals the arguments into the interpreter's words and runs the body there, made before any is needed (once the range is full it cannot be compiled either), for `lazy_compile` to return when the compile fails.","files":null}]}